  "Born Harriet Elizabeth Prescott in Calais, Maine, on 3 April 1835; she was educated at the Pinkerton Academy and made her name with 'In a Cellar' in the Atlantic Monthly in 1859. The Amber Gods and Other Stories (1863) followed, and she wrote steadily for the magazines for the rest of her life. She married Richard Spofford in 1865 and died at Deer Island, Massachusetts, on 14 August 1921.",
  "Harriet Prescott Spofford was the most brilliant of the American magazine story-writers of the 1860s and one of the finest prose stylists of the American nineteenth century: The Amber Gods created the ornamental, sensuous American tale, and her Gothic stories of New England brought the decadent style into American fiction.",
  "The ornate, jewelled prose of the magazine tale; the Gothic situation in a New England setting; the sensuous description and the delayed revelation; the supernatural explained and unexplained; the frame narrative.",
