  "Novelist and short-story writer; the first writer to make the Southern mountaineer a subject of American literature.",
  "Born Mary Noailles Murfree at Murfreesboro, Tennessee, on 24 January 1850; lame from childhood, she spent her summers in the mountains of east Tennessee and published In the Tennessee Mountains under the name Charles Egbert Craddock in 1884. The Prophet of the Great Smoky Mountains (1885), The Despot of Broomsedge Cove (1889) and The Frontiersmen (1904) followed. She died at Murfreesboro on 31 July 1922.",
  "Mary Noailles Murfree was the first writer to make the Southern mountaineer a subject of American literature: her Tennessee mountain stories of 1884 created the genre, and her dialect, her landscape and her feud narratives prepared the way for the Southern Renaissance and for the Appalachian novel.",
  "The mountain dialect rendered phonetically; the landscape of the Great Smokies as a character; the feud and the clan; the frame of the outsider visitor; the tragic ending without consolation.",
