  "Born Elizabeth Drew Barstow in Mattapoisett, Massachusetts, on 6 May 1823; she married the poet Richard Henry Stoddard in 1851 and moved to New York, where she wrote for the magazines. The Morgesons (1862), Two Men (1865) and Temple House (1867) followed, with Poems (1895). She died in New York on 1 August 1902.",
  "Elizabeth Stoddard wrote The Morgesons, one of the strangest and most original American novels of the nineteenth century and a direct ancestor of the modern novel of female desire: her heroines' refusal of the marriage plot and her abrupt, dislocated style made her a writer rediscovered by feminist criticism and now recognised as a major American novelist.",
